I see the issue! When `len == 0` and we get EOF, we're still returning a line with an empty string. The condition `if (len > 0 || feof(fp))` is always true when we hit EOF because `feof(fp)` will be set. But then we return an empty buffer which gets processed again. The issue is that after reading the last newline, we get another EOF but we're still returning a line.

The problem is: when we read "hello\n", we get h,e,l,l,o,\n and break on \n. Then we return buf with len=5. But then we loop again and fgetc returns EOF immediately because we're at the end of file. Since `len == 0` but `feof(fp)` is true, we break out and return an empty string.

The fix: when we get EOF with len==0, we should NOT return a line - we should return NULL to signal end of input:
